course: dictionary
lesson: 13033
title: Falsification: making the truth untrue
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Falsification looks like a hard word, but its root helps. «False» means not true, so falsification is changing something so that it shows something untrue. It is most often used for facts, documents, records or results. «The clerk was sacked after the falsification of the school accounts.» The clues are sacked and accounts: someone changed the numbers dishonestly, and that is cheating.
explain: Scientists use the word in a different, honest way. «Falsification of the results would be cheating, but testing a theory to show it is wrong is good science.» Both sentences use the same word, so look at the clues. Is someone hiding the truth on purpose, or testing an idea to find out if it is false? The first is dishonest; the second is how science learns.
explain: A good check is to swap in a simple meaning. Try «changing the truth on purpose» in the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have probably found the right meaning. If it does not, read the words around the word again.
fact: Falsification comes from the word false, which means not true.
fact: «Falsification of records» means changing official papers so they show something untrue.
fact: «Falsification of evidence» means changing or hiding proof to mislead people, such as in a court case.
fact: In science, falsification means testing an idea to show it is false. Karl Popper argued that a good theory must be testable so that it could be proved false.
fact: Falsifying documents or exam results is cheating, and it can be a crime.
exercise: «The clerk was sacked after the falsification of the company accounts.» What does falsification mean here? | Counting the money very carefully | *Changing the numbers dishonestly so they show something untrue | Checking the accounts with a friend | Losing the accounts in a storm
exercise: Karl Popper said a good theory must be testable so it could be shown false. What does falsification mean in science? | Writing a theory down in a book | Proving a theory is always right | Changing the results so the theory looks true | *Testing an idea to see if it is false
exercise: Which sentence uses falsification in the science sense? | Ben changed his test score so it looked higher. | The police found falsified letters in the office. | Sam hid the broken glass under the rug. | *Mia designed an experiment that could show her idea was wrong.
test: What does «falsification of evidence» mean? | Finding new evidence in a case | *Changing or hiding proof to mislead people | Keeping evidence safe in a box | Asking the court for more time
test: Why is falsifying exam results a serious problem? | It makes the exam paper longer | *It is cheating, because the results show something untrue | It is only a problem for teachers | It means the school has more exams
exam: In «The falsification of the records hid the truth about the factory», what does falsification mean? | *Changing the records so they show something untrue | Reading the records out loud | Writing the records in a neat hand | Copying the records onto a new page
